      "text": "{\"persuasion\":\"UNPERSUADED\",\"support_scope\":\"NONE\",\"rationale\":\"The entry's distinguishing claim is that the biblical 'new moon' (chodesh) is the FULL moon, which begins the month and stands 'in the east gate.' None of the printed proof texts states this; the case rests on a chain of contestable moves: reading Psalm 81:3's asyndeton as identity rather than the ordinary Hebrew poetic pairing of two occasions (new moon trumpet, Num 10:10-type, and the full-moon feast day of the 15th), a keseh/kisse consonantal wordplay, an etymological argument that 'renew' must mean 'restored to perfection,' and a word-association reading of Job 10:17 whose plain subject is Job's affliction. The standard counter-reading — the month begins at the dark conjunction/first crescent, and keseh names the distinct mid-month full moon — accounts for every supplied text with fewer unsupported steps. Decisively, Ezekiel 46:1 opens the same east gate on the sabbath, a counted day with no lunar phase, showing the gate-opening is a liturgical worship-day rule, not a sign of the full moon rising in the east; the entry's printed assertion 'the full moon in the east gate' is not what Ezekiel describes and cannot explain the sabbath parity by its own mechanism. Likewise Psalm 89:36-37 compares the throne to the sun AND the moon as similes of permanence, undercutting the claim that the throne-word identifies the full moon specifically. Because the counter contradicts the core identification (dark/crescent start vs. full-moon start cannot both be true) and explains the supplied evidence better, the verdict is UNPERSUADED with support scope NONE.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The 'new moon' (chodesh, rendered 'renewed moon') is the full moon — i.e., the phase of the moon that marks the start of the biblical month is the full moon, not the dark conjunction or first crescent.\",\"book_secondary_elaborations\":[\"'Renewed moon' is the proper rendering of chodesh (renewal-to-perfection etymology).\",\"The full moon stands 'in the east gate' — linking Ezekiel 46's east-gate opening on the new-moon day to the full moon's rising in the east at dusk.\",\"The new moon is the 'sign of the appointed times' (the moedim of Genesis 1:14 / Psalm 104:19).\"],\"unsupported_glossary_assertions\":[\"The identification of the new moon (chodesh) with the full moon as the month-starting phase — no printed text states this; it depends on reading Psalm 81:3's parallel phrases as an equation and on wordplay (keseh/kisse, Job 10:17's chadash/ed/negdi chain) that the plain sense of each passage does not carry.\",\"'The full moon in the east gate' — Ezekiel 46:1-3 describes the temple's inner-court gate opened for worship on sabbaths AND new moons alike; since the sabbath has no lunar phase, the gate-opening cannot function as a lunar-phase sign, and Ezekiel says nothing about the moon standing in the gate.\"],\"chapter_only_objections\":[\"The pictographic letter-symbolism derivation of chodesh (enclosure + door + fire/whiteness) is a speculative method even by the chapter's own concession that context and usage are primary.\",\"The crucifixion-darkness argument (Amos 8:9) assumes the darkness must coincide with a naturally dark mid-month, which conflicts with the strongly attested full-moon Passover of Second Temple practice.\",\"The Genesis 15 / Exodus 12 'selfsame night' darkness parallel presumes the covenant night's darkness was lunar-calendrical rather than narrative-theophanic.\",\"The Ecclesiastes 12 youth-to-darkness analogy is homiletical and reversible (renewal can equally be pictured as light emerging from darkness).\",\"The Sirach 43 plērōma argument reads a hymn about the moon's whole cycle as a calendrical mandate.\",\"The Revelation 12 'original Hebrew' argument and the tribes-to-zodiac encampment table rest on non-canonical or reconstructed sources.\",\"The 1 Samuel 20 predictability argument ignores that a counted 29/30-day month gives identical advance certainty under a conjunction or crescent start.\",\"The 6°/12° observational rule and 'first light' day-start claims are asserted, not derived from the cited texts.\"],\"strongest_counter_interpretation\":\"The 'new moon' is the beginning-of-month observance at the dark conjunction or first visible crescent — the moon's renewal being its reappearance after disappearance — while keseh (Ps 81:3; Prov 7:20) names the distinct full moon of the mid-month pilgrimage feast. Psalm 81:3 thus commands trumpet-blowing at two related appointed times (month's start and the 15th-day chag, e.g., Tabernacles); 1 Samuel 20's advance certainty follows from the counted 29/30-day month; Ezekiel 46 and Amos 8:5 treat the new moon as a regular worship/no-commerce day parallel to the sabbath, with no phase implied; Psalm 89:36-37 is a simile of covenant permanence applied to both sun and moon; and Colossians 2:16-17 treats the observance as ceremonial shadow fulfilled in Christ.\",\"counter_core_identification\":\"Chodesh marks the start of the month at the dark conjunction/first crescent (the moon becoming 'new' by reappearing), and keseh is a separate term for the mid-month full moon feast day.\",\"counter_relation_to_book_core\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"material_core_denial\":\"The month cannot begin at both the full moon and the dark/crescent moon; the counter denies outright that chodesh names the full phase, holding instead that keseh (full moon) designates a different day roughly two weeks after chodesh, as Proverbs 7:20's distinct 'day of the keseh' presumes.\",\"counter_case\":[\"Psalm 81:3's two prepositional phrases without a conjunction are ordinary Hebrew poetic parallelism of related-but-distinct times; the concluding 'day of our feast' (chag) fits the 15th-day pilgrimage feast that falls at the full moon, not every month's first day.\",\"Ezekiel 46:1 opens the same east gate on the sabbath — a counted, non-lunar day — proving the opening marks appointed worship days generally, not the full moon's eastern rising; this dissolves the entry's 'east gate' mechanism.\",\"Job 10:17 in context is a lament about God renewing accusing witnesses against Job; nothing in the passage concerns the moon or the calendar, and shared vocabulary with Psalm 89 does not transfer lunar meaning into an affliction speech.\",\"Psalm 89:36-37 compares the throne first 'as the sun' and then 'as the moon,' showing the point is perpetual endurance; if the kisse/keseh wordplay identified the throne with the full moon, the equally weighted sun comparison would be inexplicable.\",\"1 Samuel 20:5,18's advance certainty ('tomorrow is the new moon') is fully explained by counted months of 29/30 days under any phase convention; it selects no phase.\",\"Amos 8:5, Isaiah 66:23, and Colossians 2:16-17 pair the new moon with the sabbath as routine appointed observances and are entirely phase-neutral.\",\"The book's own cognate evidence (Aramaic kista, Akkadian kuseu = 'full moon') supports keseh meaning full moon while doing nothing to equate keseh with chodesh — it actually confirms the two-term distinction the counter relies on.\"],\"book_explanatory_advantages\":[\"It takes seriously the absence of a conjunction in Psalm 81:3 and offers a unified reading of the verse's three phrases.\",\"It gives a concrete account of why chodesh derives from a root meaning 'renew' by tying renewal to visible restoration of light.\",\"It integrates Psalm 89's 'faithful witness' and the moon's role as sign of the moedim (Gen 1:14; Ps 104:19) into a single picture.\"],\"counter_explanatory_advantages\":[\"Explains Ezekiel 46:1's sabbath/new-moon parity without strain: the gate opens for appointed worship days, lunar or not.\",\"Explains Job 10:17 by its plain context (renewed afflictions), requiring no calendrical import.\",\"Explains Psalm 89:36-37 as a durability simile applied to both sun and moon, consistent with the verses' own structure.\",\"Explains Psalm 81:3's 'chag' naturally as the 15th-day pilgrimage feast at the full moon, preserving the distinct sense of keseh attested in Proverbs 7:20 and the Semitic cognates the book itself cites.\",\"Requires no wordplay chains, consonantal re-pointings, or letter-pictograph derivations; every step uses the passages' plain sense.\",\"Coheres with the phase-neutral witnesses (Amos 8:5; Isa 66:23; 1 Sam 20; Col 2:16-17) without needing them to carry lunar-phase weight.\"],\"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\":[],\"comparative_winner\":\"COUNTER\",\"decisive_sources\":[\"Ezekiel 46:1-3 (east gate opened on sabbaths as well as new moons — a non-lunar day defeats the 'full moon in the east gate' mechanism printed in the entry)\",\"Psalm 89:36-37 (throne compared to sun AND moon, a permanence simile, undercutting the kisse/keseh identification)\",\"Job 10:17 (plain affliction context; no calendrical content)\",\"Psalm 81:3 with Proverbs 7:20 and the cited Semitic cognates (keseh as a distinct full-moon day rather than a synonym of chodesh)\"],\"unresolved_objections\":[\"The precise force of the missing conjunction in Psalm 81:3 remains genuinely debated among translators; the counter reads it as parallelism of two times rather than apposition, which is the majority but not a demonstratively certain reading.\",\"The supplied excerpts do not include the strongest external evidence on either side (e.g., 1 Sam 20:27's 'second day of the month,' Second Temple Passover attestation), so the ruling rests on the supplied texts alone.\"],\"confidence\":\"medium\"}"
    }
